  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $70.8B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs made 61,205 awards, with an average award size of $1.2M, and obligations fell 9.6% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations moved from $47.7B in FY21 to $56.4B in FY22, $61.7B in FY23, $66.9B in FY24, $78.3B in FY25, and $70.8B in FY26. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers is the largest NAICS category with $29.4B, followed by Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing with $11.1B and Offices of Physicians (except Mental Health Specialists) with $7.6B. OPTUM PUBLIC SECTOR SOLUTIONS, INC. is the largest vendor with $19.9B, followed by MCKESSON CORPORATION with $10.8B and TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP with $9.8B. The Department of Veterans Affairs continues to concentrate spending in health, pharmaceuticals, and related services while maintaining computing and construction purchases at smaller but notable levels.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are VA contract obligations?",
        "body": "Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers receives $29.4B. Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing receives $11.1B and Offices of Physicians (except Mental Health Specialists) receives $7.6B. The top three NAICS categories together account for the largest shares of FY2026 to date obligations, while Computer Systems Design Services and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction appear at $3.5B and $2.7B respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which suppliers and industries capture the most VA spending?",
        "body": "OPTUM PUBLIC SECTOR SOLUTIONS, INC. receives $19.9B and MCKESSON CORPORATION receives $10.8B. TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP receives $9.8B and QTC MEDICAL SERVICES INC receives $2.2B. Several vendors supply health services and medical products while others, including ORACLE HEALTH GOVERNMENT SERVICES, INC. and DELL FEDERAL SYSTEMS L.P, supply technology and IT services at smaller totals."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of Veterans Affairs spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $70.8B on contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs made 61,205 awards with an average award size of $1.2M, and obligations fell 9.6% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations rose from $47.7B in FY21 to $78.3B in FY25 before reaching $70.8B in FY26."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Veterans Affairs buy?",
      "a": "The Department of Veterans Affairs buys health and medical services, pharmaceuticals, physician services, IT and construction services. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers accounts for $29.4B, Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing accounts for $11.1B, and Offices of Physicians (except Mental Health Specialists) accounts for $7.6B, with Computer Systems Design Services at $3.5B and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $2.7B."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are Veterans Affairs's largest contractors?",
      "a": "OPTUM PUBLIC SECTOR SOLUTIONS, INC. is the largest contractor with $19.9B. MCKESSON CORPORATION is the second largest with $10.8B and TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP is the third largest with $9.8B. Other notable vendors include QTC MEDICAL SERVICES INC at $2.2B, OPTUMSERVE HEALTH SERVICES, INC. and LOYAL SOURCE GOVERNMENT SERVICES LLC at $1.8B each, and ORACLE HEALTH GOVERNMENT SERVICES, INC. at $1.3B."
